\details{
Supply two formulae, with the response in the first and a one-sided
dispersion formula second. Both support offsets. The mean is
\code{mu = exp(eta1)}, dispersion is \code{theta = exp(eta2)}, and the variance is
\code{mu + theta * mu^2}, using the same parameterization as \code{\link[=pig]{pig()}}.

REML supports outer Newton and BFGS optimization; NCV uses BFGS, with
default leave-one-out or neighbourhoods supplied through \code{nei}.
ML, QNCV, EFS, \code{bam()} and \code{gamm()} are outside the supported scope.
Separate coefficients are required for the predictors: the same covariates
can appear in both formulae, but shared coefficients are not supported.

Fitted values and response predictions have columns \code{mu}, \code{theta}.
Link predictions give their logarithms. Response prediction standard
errors use the delta method. The callbacks \code{rd(mu)}, \code{cdf(q, mu, logp = FALSE)} and \code{qf(p, mu)} take a two-column matrix of response-scale
parameters in that order. They condition on those parameters and retain
the probability boundaries and numerical limits documented in \code{\link[=pig]{pig()}}.

Responses must be finite nonnegative integers; weights must be finite
and nonnegative, with at least one positive count having positive weight.
Weights multiply likelihood contributions without changing individual
observation distributions. Missing rows follow \code{gam()}'s \code{na.action}.

Use \code{~ 1} to estimate constant dispersion. Its REML estimate need not
match \code{\link[=pig]{pig()}}, because dispersion is a regression coefficient here
rather than an extra family parameter. Dispersion is not artificially
bounded; near-Poisson data can give weakly identified dispersion fits.
Very large counts can be computationally expensive.

Deviance compares with the saturated mean at each fitted dispersion.
Null deviance fits a constant mean predictor with fitted dispersions and
mean offsets retained. These are conditional mean-fit diagnostics.
Mathematical details are in \code{inst/maths/pigls.md} in the package source.
